        "step": 1,
        "description": "Install and load the ggplot2 package.",
        "code": "install.packages(\"ggplot2\")\nlibrary(ggplot2)"
      },
      {
        "step": 2,
        "description": "Create a small example dataset.",
        "code": "example_data <- data.frame(\n  Category = c(\"A\", \"B\", \"C\", \"D\"),\n  Value = c(10, 20, 15, 25)\n)"
      },
      {
        "step": 3,
        "description": "Create the bar chart using ggplot2.",
        "code": "ggplot(data = example_data, aes(x = Category, y = Value)) +\n  geom_bar(stat = \"identity\", fill = \"steelblue\") +\n  labs(title = \"Example Bar Chart\", x = \"Category\", y = \"Value\") +\n  theme_minimal()"
      }
    ],
    "explanation": "In the code above:\n- `example_data` is a simple data frame with two columns: `Category` and `Value`.\n- `ggplot()` initializes the plot, specifying the dataset and aesthetics (`x` and `y` variables).\n- `geom_bar(stat = \"identity\")` creates a bar chart where the height of the bars corresponds directly to the `Value` column.\n- `fill = \"steelblue\"` sets the color of the bars.\n- `labs()` adds a title and axis labels.\n- `theme_minimal()` applies a clean, minimal theme to the plot.",
    "output_description": "The output will be a bar chart with four bars representing the values for categories A, B, C, and D."
  }
}
